  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe an **unstable** equilibrium point.{{field-1}} solutions that start\n{{field-2}}\nthe equilibrium\n{{field-3}}\nthe equilibrium.",
  "worked_solution": "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{\u2217}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{\u2217},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}\u2286O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{\u2217}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{\u2217}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{\u2217}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{\u2217}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n$\\text{Some}$ solutions that start $\\text{arbitrarily close to}$ the equilibrium $\\text{eventually move away from}$ the equilibrium.",
